WHAT: Adds ArrayView<T, N> to fe core's ABI module. ArrayView stores a byte
offset into ABI-encoded calldata and provides lazy, zero-copy element access.
Instead of decoding all N elements into memory upfront, it reads elements
on demand directly from calldata.

WHY: Fe's Decode for [T; N] did [first; N] (32 MSTORE ops to init) then
overwrote elements 1..N-1 in a loop (31 more MSTOREs). That's 31 provably
dead stores per array decode. ArrayView eliminates ALL of them — no memory
allocation, no stores, just direct calldataload at the computed offset.

GAS IMPACT: Merkle (which uses a 32-element proof array) improved dramatically:
  Before (step 0 baseline): computeRoot=20,702  verify=36,525
  After (step 17):          computeRoot=9,971   verify=14,810
  Delta:                    -10,731 (-52%)       -21,715 (-59%)

Note: This combines ArrayView (this step) with prior unchecked changes
(steps 15-16). The ArrayView-specific contribution is the elimination of
~64 dead MSTOREs (init + overwrite) per proof decode.

Other contracts: no change (they don't use array parameters).

CODEGEN IMPACT: Merkle IR drops from ~200 obj.store to ~10. Function count
may change as decode helpers are no longer needed.

TEAM NOTES: This is the single largest gas improvement in the entire campaign.
It validates the principle that Fe has zero deployed contracts — breaking
the ABI decode API to add a fundamentally better abstraction is the right
call. ArrayView is a language-level design innovation, not an optimizer
tweak. The team should look for similar opportunities where the std library
generates structurally wasteful IR.

The ABI generator doesn't map ArrayView to Solidity's uint256[32] yet
(ABI generation fails with a type mismatch error), but the bytecode is
correct and forge tests pass. ABI compat is a separate fix.
